{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1849"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c155): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c155.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1849"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge music-stage/pop-rock, music-stage/pop-rock","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/music-stage__pop-rock.g4.json quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/zz-r2-music-stage__pop-rock.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",715],["+"," The robot helmets – silver for Bangalter, gold for Homem-Christo – had a cinematic ancestor: Bangalter called Brian De Palma's 1974 rock musical \"Phantom of the Paradise\", whose hero hides behind a mask, the foundation for a lot of what the duo was about artistically."],["=",1],["+","Solo stars have played the same game. David Bowie moved from the glam-rock alien Ziggy Stardust to the Thin White Duke, a cold, immaculately dressed aristocrat in white shirt and black waistcoat whom he first tried out in late 1974 and who is named in the title track of the 1976 album „Station to Station“. Bowie stopped performing as the Duke after leaving Los Angeles for Berlin at the end of 1976.\n\n"],["=",171]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",691],["+"," Die Roboterhelme – silbern für Bangalter, golden für Homem-Christo – hatten ein filmisches Vorbild: Bangalter nannte Brian De Palmas Rockmusical „Phantom im Paradies (Phantom of the Paradise)“ von 1974, dessen Held sich hinter einer Maske verbirgt, die Grundlage für vieles, worum es dem Duo künstlerisch ging."],["=",1],["+","Auch Solostars spielten dieses Spiel. David Bowie wechselte vom Glamrock-Außerirdischen Ziggy Stardust zum Thin White Duke, einem kühlen, makellos gekleideten Aristokraten in weißem Hemd und schwarzer Weste, den er Ende 1974 erstmals erprobte und der im Titelstück des Albums „Station to Station“ von 1976 beim Namen genannt wird. Nachdem Bowie Ende 1976 Los Angeles in Richtung Berlin verlassen hatte, trat er nicht mehr als Duke auf.\n\n"],["=",163]]}]},
